cartridge "HEX WAR/TODD HEIMARCK, COMPUTE!/1986"

' HEX WAR
' Based on "Hex War" by Todd Heimarck, COMPUTE! July 1986: a two-player
' strategy game for the Commodore 128, with versions for the 64 and the
' Atari (Kevin Mykytyn), the Apple II (Tim Victor), the IBM PC/PCjr
' (Patrick Parrish) and the Amiga (Philip Nelson). Made for the
' ColecoVision in Amy (the language by Amy Bienvenu) by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic) for Amy Studio, 2026.
'
' Not a line-for-line port: a ColecoVision game on the listings' rules
' (orig/ has the scans; the Amiga listing, the most readable, was the
' main guide). Kept: 61 hexes (T, B from 0 to 8 with T + B from 4 to 12),
' twelve cities from the listings' DATA, four robot armies of strength
' 64 (hex 40) a side, three movement points (1 to step, 1 more into a
' neutral or enemy hex), zones of control, merging (one of the two
' armies under 32), battles between neighbouring armies (KA = KB = 1/48,
' KC = 1/32 of an army's strength, split by six coin tosses), injured
' bots to the botspital (back in five turns, or captured for seven),
' disrupted bots back next turn or the turn after, a reinforcement queue
' of 21 turns a side made at random as the listing does, income of a bot
' per hex owned, new armies entering at the corners (lost when the enemy
' owns the entry hex, waiting while it is occupied), and the five
' scenarios. Changed, each noted where it happens: a battle cannot take
' more bots than an army has (the listing let strengths go below zero and
' made injured bots out of nothing); values are bytes (a merge over 255
' is refused; a queue slot spills once into the next).
' Made for this machine: the honeycomb in big two-colour hexes, the
' owner's colour, cities, capitals, robots, "++" for an army done, its
' strength in hexadecimal; a status panel with each number in hex and in
' decimal (to learn hexadecimal, the Amiga's speech idea); laser bursts
' in battle; a computer opponent; music under play.
'
' Controls: the stick moves the hex cursor. Left and right go to the
' next hex in the row; the diagonals go to the four slanted neighbours
' (up-right NE, up-left NW, down-left SW, down-right SE); straight up or
' down zigzags NE/NW (or SW/SE), so you can climb a column. The left
' button picks up your army, moves it to the next hex under the cursor,
' or (on its own hex) stops it ("++") or puts it back if it has not
' moved. The right button ends your turn. Blue is player one (joystick
' 1), red player two (joystick 2, or the computer).
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22; tiles from code
' 128; the frame loop is protected from the NMI and every screen is drawn
' with the NMI off after a wait.

const TO0t0empty = 128
const TO0t0city = 129
